Notes.

Note:  When PTUTIL is released, RETAIN this section.  It has a lot of good
stuff unrelated to PTUTIL in the short-term, etc. [Brain-stormings 'R Us].


Next:

Check for no files. - CHECK!

Check for mixed files.- CHECK!

Check for non-unique files.- CHECK!

leave room for check if /E that files are blocks past the end of the TFS
directory and thus have a definite highest block.  For now, the highest
block is 7777 if /E. - CHECK!

This will involve for Version 8Z reading in the extended-length files directory
which still exists [will change in 9A to be the single-entry per logical unit
places at the end of the systems-program directory to eliminate the extended-length
files directory entirely, since no longer will there be more than the single file
per logical unit, and the SET command will have added some maintenance functions
such as the name so the user can prevent anomaly, yet can customize the name freely.

The recommended name for the extended-length file will be XFILEx where x is the
logical unit thus XFILE0 through XFILE7.  Properly documented this should make sense.

A separate issue is then the ability to finish PAL /X because we can then have a
series of extended-length work files to contain the raw references, and the
PAL /X processing overlay can access the same info [which will be a small table
in the end of the systems program directory to indicate the disposition of the
extended files for PAL /X purposes.  Still need to come up with syntax for this,
but something like

SET EXTEND{ed} NAME foobar:0 for 0 through 7.

And SET SCR1 0 [0-7]
SET SCR2 1 [0-7] etc.

SET SCR1 CLEAR
SET SCR2 CLEAR [or maybe OFF?]

Each attempt to set one has to check that it is already clear first or reject
the attempt.

But that way, PAL can have up to eight scratch extended-length files to output the
references to.  Assuming the best part of 4096 blocks times eight units on a good
size disk-based system [RK with 4 packs] that's 32,000 blocks in the basic system
and compare that to crummy PAL8 that can only do the equivalent of 8192 worst case
assuming they have 4096 record directories [which are double-blocks thus 8192
max blocks to hold xref data, etc.] ["mammoth option?  hah! :-) ]


But for the present, for all extended-length file purposes, the "dangerous mode"
applies where it is really a TFS file in the TFS directory as a starting point
that extends to the proper end-block as defined in the extended-length directory.
This should be the exact same mechanism as in OS8CON [check, but ought to be
totally "liftable" as is; if not, make it so and indicate OS8CON needs a
"facelift" even before committing to version 9A.

And make it abundantly clear that if the last possible TFS slot is always used as
the faux extended-length file [under the restriction] this is only 16 blocks
different from the effective value once the extended-length entry method is
adopted.  Moreover, you can move it down into the normally TFS area to make
extended-length files meaningful on the standard release without any way to
manage the directory sizes without patching. [Sort of a "feature" that you can
decide dynamically just how much to use as an extended-length file [faux or
otherwise] and later change it back to pure TFS usage as intended.  When you have
multiple logical devices, this really matters.  [But in the future there should
be a nice resource-management configuration-type utility to set the divvying up
of resources, and as a side issue, can also move around the system programs, etc.]

The weak side of the basic system is that I or a trained person can configure
whatever you want, but there is no utility to do it, and users are uncomfortable
about what they really don't understand.  As a practical matter, once set to a
practical value, there is only the slightest chance of ever again changing it.

But no "comfort zone" in the current limitation, despite the fact that in the end
the utility is only used transiently as a rule, etc.



Still to be done:

At various development phases, make some assumptions about appropriate page zero
literals and variables/temporaries.  Once the program is done, it is perfectly fine
to "waste" some page zero space because this is essentially a closed-end project.

You reach a point of little to be done, and most importantly, little to be done that
changes the overall core-size of the result!  As such, if page zero is used so
minimally, it would make sense to eliminate current-page stuff if it helps
to pack pages better, and then fill in with text messages to really fill them, etc.
all in the effort to make the final overall core size as small as practical.

Kick around the notion of @ and ` -> # as a new standard of conversion [instead of
just throwing them away].  # is an underutilized character, although P?S/8 PAL
has an extension to equate # with ( in literals.  [But no way to optionally close
except still with the ).  Macro-11 uses the #, but there is no equivalent notation
in -11 code for an optional trailer.

Other than that, and literal usage of # for text strings wherever, it has no
present usage.

It would be useful to make sure that [eventually if it has to be modified] that
the statement

           TEXT  #I AM THE TEXT STRING#

works, that would solve the problem of the few people who use [without knowledge of
the underlying issues] @.  [Note: DIAL-MS has similar issues with the ' character
but in P/S/8 that's a non-issue.]

CONFIRMED:  P?S/8 PAL supports it fine.

If adopted, the manual section about the standard conversion needs to add a footnote
that covers conversion programs.  OS8CON would be upgraded to be compatible [and
as a result, the DIAL -> P?S/8 convertor [L6DCON] as well to be consistent].

[Note:  This is an 8Z revision/build thing as well; need not wait until 9A.]

Very important:  Need to be able to generate an entire system soon to avoid doing
all of this in patches; needs to be source changes!  Thus, needs to be a new
collection of files that obsoletes old ones as necessary, etc.]

Towards that end, eliminate the stable device-dependent source files and then
just include binaries in the "A" and "B" areas as long as this overall method of
system generation applies.  Thus, an "other" area as well for the seldom-updated
stuff.  Also, the various patches need to be updated [such as TDCOPY and DTFRMT etc.
so that these programs are NOT part of the general release, and as such live on a
non-booted logical unit as binaries [and likely small BATCH files] that allow these
seldom-used things to be compatible with the slight limitation of the R % [$]
mechanism of must start at 00200.  [So make an internal patch at 00200 to
JMP to the new code, and then restore what that was before, then start in
memory, etc.]  Thus, all newer releases of such as those will always start at 00200.

This also means rethink the starting block of TFS and ending block of the system
programs directory for a better balance.  On the TC01/TC08 system this means that
DTCOPY and DTFRMT will be remove which makes it easier to be shorter by say
8K usage which amounts to an increase of four TFS slots available.  [A nice
value to change, say from 0600 to 0500.  Again, something for the build/revision
release change to reflect this.  The change-log will be "busy" with all of this!

OS/8 TC01/08 systems NEVER put these images on the system device, but are on a DTA1:
typically.  And we CAN have a programs-heavy non-system device as yet another
alternative anyway! [Thus, for these and later the TD8E "cousins"] this can be
a configuration choice in terms of released system images.  Lean and mean versus
"heavy" with seldom-needed programs OR just add the TFS binary and BATCH file
equivalents on a system take DESIGNATED to be "heavy".  More choices is always
better if explained.

This also includes the notion of why RKLFMT [or whatever; isn't that L a never-was
intended in-house feature?] should be RK4MAT because so few people ever will see
an RK08/RK01 system.  All depends on what Western Union does with their dead
inventory; the stupid company holds onto old equipment for decades, but as they
become more and more of a "ghost" [and are they much more than a money-gram
company anymore?  At some point, that inventory had to be dealt with, and hopefully
was not scrapped for metal weight!] [P?S/8 was never written for it, but it is so
easy to do, vague cousin of RK8E from the P?S/8 point of view, just different
logistics to address to the nearest 1/2 record and of course requires a system
device handler extension likely EASIER to implement than the RK8E which of course
doesn't even challenge the newer definition of same, etc.]
  In any case, I suspect the RK01 format program likely was called "RK4MAT" and why
OS/8 chose RKLFMT if for no other reason but to distinguish them in the release,
thus even though there was eventually no hardware reason, the name stuck, etc.

We can ponder that at some future point, but perhaps imitating OS/ on this tiny
name convention point is not the worst thing.  [Just document the name stands
for a variation that never happened in production, similar to the RX03 never
happened in DEC production, yet OS/8 and P?S/8 RX01 both support it!.]  [Or the
never-released TC8E!]




It should be added now to PTUTIL, update the documentation as a footnote that SOME
