Up-coming issue:

the source code of PTUTIL respects BSGRP=6.  All the rest of the logged patches
update things to the equivalent where it applies [DATE, PAL, SYSTAT, PRINT, etc.]

But a/o 01-Jan-2016, we can go for BSGRP=7 if we want to.

a)  Leave it alone and the date "dies" [meaning unpatched, but always patchable]
in 2019.

b) Upgrade the existing patches to BSGRP=7 equivalent, and PTUTIL must agree,
and as a result version 8Z [the revision/build can play a nice role in this!]
gets that Version 8Z to "die" in 2027 [again, can always be patched well into the
next century!].

It is doubtful that PTUTIL will be done before then, thus this consideration, etc.




Silent running [/S] mode means no reports other than forced echo on
error messages.  All initial error message will always echo and abort.

IF echo on, version opening, one-liner like OS8CON about what it does. - NOW
AVAILABLE TO BE CALLED BY SCRIBE FOLLOWED BY A CALL TO CRLF IF NECESSARY.

PTUTIL V01A.

THE ABOVE IS CALLED "PROGMSG".

Report count of output files created if in that direction.  Paper-tape
could have frame count printed.  Only do this if verbose mode, files created, frames
read or frames punched of punching

FRAMES READ DOES NOT INCLUDE IGNORED NULL CHARACTERS OR TRAILING CONTROL-Z SINCE THE
TRAILING CHARACTER COULD BE CONTRIVED BY TIME-OUT AND THE REST OF THE PROGRAM DOESN'T
CONCERN ITSELF WITH THAT NUANCE [NOR DOES IT REALLY MATTER].  IF THERE REALLY IS
MORE TAPE TO READ, IT COULD BE THE RESULT OF A TANGLE OR SOME ODD FORMATTING.  IN
EITHER CASE, MULTIPLE TAPES HAVE TO BE READ.  IF THE USER SUSPECTS THIS CAN HAPPEN,
THE MULTIPLE TAPES OPTION SHOULD BE USED; ANSWER YES OR NO [AND THE LAST TIME NO] TO
HANDLE THIS;  WITHOUT THE MULTIPLE TAPE OPTION, THE PROGRAM IS REALLY DESIGNED TO
HANDLE THE IDEAL AND SINGULAR CASE OF THE SIMH PTRFILE INPUT, BUT REAL-WORLD
PAPER-TAPE READING CAN BE TROUBLESOME.  THE USER HAS TO DECIDE WHETHER TO CONTINUE
WITH ADDITIONAL TAPES [LOGICALLY TO THE PROGRAM] OR TO START ALL OVER AGAIN.  PTUTIL
[WHEN DONE] PROVIDES SUFFICIENT OPTIONS TO HANDLE ANY SITUATION.]  [NOTE:  ALL FRAMES
ARE SEVEN-BIT IGNORING THE PARITY BIT.]  IT IS POSSIBLE TRAILING JUNK MAY WIND UP
IN THE CONVERSION, BUT SINCE THERE IS NO HARD AND FAST RULE ABOUT NECESSARILY USING
CONTROL-Z TO TERMINATE THE STRING, THE USER HAS TO HAND EDIT THE RESULT IF NEED BE.
PTUTIL SETS A GOOD EXAMPLE BY OUTPUTTING A NEW PAPER-TAPE WITH LEADER WITH PARITY
BIT SET, A SIMILAR TRAILER AND A FINAL CONTROL-Z.  IF THE TAPE IS IN THAT FORMAT, IT
WILL NEVER BE A PROBLEM, BUT TAKING PEOPLE OUT OF THE STONE AGE OF PAPER-TAPE YOU
CAN ENCOUNTER WHO KNOWS WHAT PROBLEMS.  BETTER TO HAVE THE USER HAVE TO REMOVE JUNK
THAN PREVENT THE ABILITY TO CREATE A FULL CONVERSION WITH SOME MINOR EFFORT.


Double-precision decimal print with leading zeroes suppressed. - CHECK!

89900 Characters Punched

89900 Characters Read

Echo the input from the tape option switch with control-O support
what option switch for the above?  /D.

/D - Display paper-tape input during conversion to P?S/8 output files.

Figure out how to manage low-speed input .  Time-out stops the read.
In time-out mode, more tapes messages and can abort during the prompt.


TTY: input for paper-tapes:

1)  No time-out [disallow on DECmates!]

2) Control-Z ends the input [of this tape]  [/M allow multiple tapes, ask for
it; mutually exclusive if /S silent running mode.

3) control-O flips echo of input.  An option switch [/D?] sets initial echo; default
is don't echo, just read in and convert.

4) Control-A, Control-B, <BRK> abort, Control-C sets logical abort bit and aborts.
KRS should allow pass back of the control-C status as well, but not critical because
software-abort will be set.
Control-O detected *ALWAYS* prints ^O.

Control-Z ends the input.  It could be on the tape; if there is no more tape to read
user must type control-Z manually.


If the logical console is enabled, manual control-Z is to console.  Same for <BRK>
and control-A, B, C stuff as well, and in fact all including control-O.

But the basic character input is physical hardware.

Warning:  May not work if console overlay enabled [and to same hardware!].


